   sites that look the address up. Direct compiled calls and cfg-named dispatch
   targets bypass it. `hle_func` is resolved at generation time and does not
   have this gap.
+
+
+## Optional guarded fast path
+
+`hle_guard_func <pc16> <C_identifier>` adds a try-handler to existing native
+variants without adding analysis roots, replacing the original body, or
+manufacturing modes. It cannot share an entry with `hle_func` or
+`hle_spc_upload`. `--no-hle` disables it. Active handlers and their eligible
+modes are recorded in `program_manifest.json` as `hle_guards`; cfg and emitter
+identity also remain part of the generated cache key.
+
+```c
+bool TryHandler(CpuState *cpu, uint16_t entry_s, uint8_t hrv,
+                uint32_t host_return_pc24, RecompReturn *result);
+```
+
+The callback runs once per actual native invocation, after the first original
+watchdog/deadline checks and before charging original instructions. Arguments
+are the logical inherited entry stack, return-frame kind, and return address
+witnessed by the generated prologue. The helper must validate its supported
+ROM, context and frame before making any guest change. Returning false must
+leave guest CPU, memory and clocks untouched, allowing the original body to
+execute. Returning true owns the supported instructions' timing, guest return
+frame and resulting CPU/memory state, and fills `result`. The generated wrapper
+pops its host recomp frame exactly once, using the yield pop for an LLE unwind
+result or an active pending unwind (including propagated nested sentinels). Unsupported contexts should refuse rather than guess at frame shape.
+
+`python tests/v2/test_hle_guard_func.py` compiles and executes the actual emitted
+entry and checks true/false, inherited context, deadline ordering and unwind
+behavior. It also exercises generation identities, mode preservation, malformed
+configuration and byte-identical native fallback with `--no-hle`.
